# ── Chapter 13 fidelity pins: "The One Thing Taken" ──────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/13-the-one-thing-taken.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# TRIGGER FIRED. This file used to record "there is no release" as an absence and
# said: "If a release rule is ever added, the closing section must be rewritten."
# It was, and it has been. Release is now Article 6's `~free` guard plus Article
# 1's `free -> person`, and the closing two sections are pinned below.
#
# One claim here is still an ABSENCE and cannot be queried: "there is no duration
# in it anywhere, no term, no length, nothing that counts". `verify.sh` holds
# that one — a standalone numeric literal in an enacted line fails the build;
# digits inside typed source identifiers such as T3 are not quantities.
#
# The other: "Nothing depends on it" — travel appears in the constitution only as
# a rule head. Test the rule BODIES, since a bare grep matches the head too:
#   awk -F'->' '/^[^#]/ && /->/ && $1 ~ /travel/' new-book-plans/constitution.nibli
# must stay EMPTY. `verify.sh` runs it with a positive control.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closed the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact now aborts the run instead of loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 35

# "Hano was convicted ... and cannot move freely."
? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

? travel(Hano).
# => FALSE

# The audit, item by item — everything else Hano keeps
? person(Hano).
# => TRUE

? expresses(Hano).
# => TRUE

? decide(Hano, Ballot).
# => TRUE

? false(Hano).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, Hano).
# => FALSE

? dwell(Hano).
# => TRUE

# "the difference between Hano and Jala, who did the same thing to the same
#  person and was never convicted"
? injure(Jala, Ivo).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Jala).
# => FALSE

? travel(Jala).
# => TRUE

# Voiding is not confinement: Bela lost credibility and still moves
? false(Bela).
# => TRUE

? travel(Bela).
# => TRUE

# ── "And it may end" / "What leaving costs" ──────────────────────────────────
# LAST in the file: this block asserts a release and changes Hano for everything
# after it. Nothing follows.

# "Somebody records that it is over — that is one of the things the world may say"
free(Hano).

# "the machinery that was holding a person stops holding them. Movement returns.
#  The placement stops. The marker that was watching for isolation goes quiet."
? prisoner(Hano).
# => FALSE

? travel(Hano).
# => TRUE

? err(Hano, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# "The person keeps every entitlement they had ... and the body that owes it
#  still owes it"
? person(Hano).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Hano, event { dwell() }).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Provision, Hano).
# => TRUE

# In the supplied record both active actualities come through confinement. When
# a sentence finishes they stop; dormant recipient-side routes do not fabricate
# replacement evidence. These are the two pins that carry that narrower claim.
? expresses(Hano).
# => FALSE

? dwell(Hano).
# => FALSE

# ── The Hano/Jala lists, pinned item by item ─────────────────────────────────
# "Four differences, and three of them run the wrong way." An earlier draft said
# "Everything else on both lists is identical", which was false in four places.
# Pinned as pairs so the claim cannot drift back.
? expresses(Jala).
# => FALSE

? dwell(Jala).
# => FALSE

? fit(Jala, Homestay).
# => FALSE

? err(Jala, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# and Jala is genuinely unconvicted — the control for all four
? prisoner(Jala).
# => FALSE

# ── "What the design does not say is WHEN" — the record's door, today ────────
# Ruled 2026-08-02: the design does not say how long, and that is ratified,
# not pending. The time vocabulary is refused at assert time — the loud door
# (chapter 1's closure) is the only way in.
# T3 now represents one independently witnessed transition, two separately
# witnessed order claims, and one reviewed custody interval. These are symbolic
# record relations, not a clock or a computed term.
? succeed(Epoch_Current, Transition).
# => TRUE

? precede(Order_Court_A, EventSequence).
# => TRUE

? precede(Order_Log_Court_A, RecordSequence).
# => TRUE

? time(Window_Custody, ReviewedInterval).
# => TRUE

? correct(Case_Hano, ActivePower).
# => TRUE

# `year` and `earlier` remain unadmitted negative controls. Their refusal does
# not prove the model has no temporal path; it proves date-shaped words cannot
# bypass the reviewed path through the loud vocabulary door.
:refuse reasoning /not admitted vocabulary/
year(Term, Two).

:refuse reasoning /not admitted vocabulary/
earlier(Custody, Release).

# ── THE TERM THAT RELEASES NOBODY. MUST BE LAST: everything below persists ───
# Walk through the loud door on purpose: admit the word, state the term — no
# digit anywhere, `Two` is an opaque name — and watch what it does. Nothing.
# The conviction stands, no release derives, nothing counts down or notices a
# term run out. Ruk is the subject because this file has already released Hano
# above — the term has to be stated for somebody still held. "A term could be written into this design tomorrow and nothing
# in it would count the term down" — executed. This is what the ruling
# declined to build: a promise with no keeper.
#
# A nuance found while checking: `admits` for a FRESH name loads from a probe
# (the too-late refusal guards relations with prior facts), so this ceremony
# can be performed on top of the record from outside the constitution's text.
# In the society, the widening is still the visible reviewable act chapter 1
# describes; in this harness, it means refusal pins guard the shipped text and
# a probe may always widen on top — which is exactly what this block does.
admits("year").
year(Term_Ruk, Two).

? prisoner(Ruk).
# => TRUE

? free(Ruk).
# => FALSE
